# Lab 1 – Enterprise topology

## Objective

Create the physical and logical topology of the network i will be working on

## Network Environment

The JTEC lab environment represents a small enterprise network built using Cisco Packet Tracer. The topology uses a redundant core-and-access switching design, with multiple VLANs and Layer 3 switching implemented at the core.

The main devices include:

CORE1 – Primary Layer 3 core switch responsible for inter-VLAN routing and providing default gateways through Switch Virtual Interfaces (SVIs).
CORE2 – Secondary core switch providing network redundancy and forming an LACP EtherChannel with CORE1.
ACCESS1–ACCESS4 – Access-layer switches connecting end-user devices and extending VLANs into the access network.
End devices – PCs, laptops and servers representing users and network resources.
Routers – Used where required for WAN/routing scenarios and connectivity beyond the local switching infrastructure.

Topology Design

The network follows a simplified three-tier enterprise design, with the core switches providing the central Layer 3 infrastructure and the access switches connecting end devices.
The core switches use redundant links to improve availability. VLAN traffic is carried between switches using 802.1Q trunking, while inter-VLAN communication is handled by the Layer 3 core through SVIs.

The topology also incorporates Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) to prevent Layer 2 loops and EtherChannel/LACP to combine multiple physical links into a single logical connection between the core switches.

This design allows the labs to demonstrate both normal network operation and failure scenarios, including VLAN/trunk issues, routing problems, EtherChannel failures and STP path selection.

## Technologies

- Cisco IOS
- Packet Tracer

## Configuration

The JTEC network uses a combination of Layer 2 switching and Layer 3 routing configuration. The key configuration varies between labs, but the following represents the main configuration implemented so far.

VLAN Configuration

Multiple VLANs were created to logically segment the network:
VLAN 10 - Management
VLAN 20 - Users
VLAN 30 - Sales
VLAN 40 - Finance
VLAN 50 - Servers
VLAN 60 - Voice
VLAN 70 - Guest
VLAN 80 - NET-MGMT
VLAN 99 - Native

Trunk links were configured between the access and core switches to transport multiple VLANs:

interface GigabitEthernet1/4
 switchport mode trunk
 switchport trunk native vlan 99
 switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,30,40,50,60,70,80,99

Inter-VLAN Routing

CORE1 was configured as a Layer 3 switch:

ip routing
ipv6 unicast-routing

SVIs were configured to provide default gateways for the VLANs. For example:

interface Vlan10
 ip address 10.10.2.129 255.255.255.224
 ipv6 address 2001:D88:10:10::1/64
 no shutdown

SSH Management

Secure remote management was configured using SSH:

ip domain-name jtec.local
crypto key generate rsa
ip ssh version 2

Local authentication was enabled:

username admin privilege 15 secret <REDACTED>

VTY lines were restricted to SSH:

line vty 0 4
 login local
 transport input ssh
 exec-timeout 5 0
LACP EtherChannel

CORE1 and CORE2 were connected using two physical Gigabit Ethernet links combined into Port-Channel1 using LACP:

interface range GigabitEthernet1/6 - 7
 channel-group 1 mode active

The logical Port-Channel was then configured as a trunk:

interface Port-channel1
 switchport mode trunk
 switchport trunk native vlan 99
 switchport trunk allowed vlan 10,20,30,40,50,60,70,80,99
Spanning Tree

Spanning Tree was used to control the Layer 2 topology and prevent switching loops. Verification was performed using:

show spanning-tree
show spanning-tree vlan 20

This was particularly important when analysing the redundant CORE1/CORE2 links and determining the root port and forwarding state.

Verification Commands

Configuration was verified using Cisco IOS operational commands rather than relying solely on configuration output:

show ip interface brief
show vlan brief
show interfaces trunk
show interfaces switchport
show mac address-table
show cdp neighbors
show ip route
show etherchannel summary
show spanning-tree
ping

These commands were used to confirm interface state, VLAN membership, trunk operation, Layer 3 routing, EtherChannel formation, STP operation and end-to-end connectivity.
